ref 17592186524603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524938 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186524725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524938 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524938 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524938 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186524482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524938 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e37207bf-0563-5832-ad30-c6a23c3c930c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524966 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524966 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524966 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524966 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524966 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524966 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1741c2e1-8000-5ded-a8b9-94a357d2c64a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524966 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi A=0 \\\\ 3\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{27\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524971 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524971 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524971 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-10y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼10+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524971 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "234b7e73-d01a-5fb8-b7f2-7b7733987672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524971 #edb/ref 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})-10(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\inft